WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.190
<v Instructor>Okay, so now let's talk about</v>

2
00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:05.700
an exciting development, which is called AWS Outposts.

3
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.710
So what is hybrid cloud?

4
00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.860
Businesses that keep an on-premises infrastructure

5
00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.270
alongside a cloud infrastructure are called hybrid cloud.

6
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.130
And when we have that,

7
00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.130
we have two ways of dealing with IT systems.

8
00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:23.610
We have one for the cloud of AWS, for example,

9
00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:28.610
using the console of AWS, the CLI, and the APIs from AWS.

10
00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.100
But also we have another one that is dedicated

11
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.080
to on-premises infrastructure.

12
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.750
And so that means two different types of skill sets,

13
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.020
two different types of API and that may be complicated.

14
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.270
So AWS said, okay, we do recognize

15
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.730
that some companies will run a hybrid cloud,

16
00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:47.340
therefore let's create Outposts.

17
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.590
And Outposts are server racks that offer

18
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:53.400
the same AWS infrastructure services, API, and tools

19
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.070
to build your own application on-premises,

20
00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.540
just like in the cloud.

21
00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.400
So what this means that they will come

22
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.030
and set up and manage Outposts racks,

23
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:06.600
which are servers, within your on-premises infrastructure.

24
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.340
And then these servers come preloaded with the AWS services,

25
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.710
but you can benefit them from on-premises.

26
00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.140
So as we can see now, on your corporate data center,

27
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.340
you will have your Outposts racks directly set up by AWS.

28
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.510
And this allows you to extend the AWS services directly

29
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.400
onto your on-premises data centers,

30
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.680
which is I think, revolutionary.

31
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.380
But the difference between an EC2 instance running

32
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.240
on the cloud, and an EC2 instance running

33
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:36.780
in your own data center is that now you are responsible

34
00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:41.190
for the security, the physical security, of the rack itself,

35
00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.910
because that rack is within your own data center.

36
00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:47.850
So the benefits from using Outposts are many.

37
00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:49.950
First of all, you get low latency access

38
00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:51.570
to on-premises system.

39
00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.660
You get local data processing, so the data may actually

40
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.400
never leave your on-premises system

41
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:57.810
and never go to the cloud.

42
00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:58.980
Data residency, so again,

43
00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.960
it lives within your own data centers.

44
00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.300
It's easy, for example, to start a migration

45
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.430
from on-premises to Outposts,

46
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.220
and then when you're ready from Outposts to the cloud.

47
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.240
It's a fully managed service,

48
00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.670
so AWS will manage the service for you.

49
00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:15.630
And with Outposts for now, you can launch a lot of services,

50
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:20.630
such as Amazon EC2, Amazon EBS, Amazon S3, Amazon EKS,

51
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.810
Amazon ECS, Amazon RDS, and Amazon EMR.

52
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.170
So it's really a cool way to extend the cloud directly

53
00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.430
onto your own infrastructure on-premises system.

54
00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:33.720
And this is revolutionary.

55
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.890
So let's take the example of Amazon S3 on AWS Outposts.

56
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.170
So you use the S3 APIs to actually store

57
00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.250
and retrieve data locally on AWS Outposts.

58
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.750
This way you keep your data close

59
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.550
to your on-premises application

60
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.970
and you reduce data transfers to AWS regions.

61
00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.160
So there is a specific storage class

62
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.940
for Outposts on S3 called S3 Outposts.

63
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.820
The default and encryption scheme for anything on Outposts

64
00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.800
is going to be SSE-S3.

65
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.330
And so how is the data accessed from Outposts onto AWS?

66
00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.880
Well, you have S3 on Outposts and you can build

67
00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.460
an S3 access point on it.

68
00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.010
And then thanks to this, you can manage security,

69
00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.770
and make sure that an easy to instance in your VPC

70
00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:21.540
can access your S3 on Outposts through this S3 access point.

71
00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:24.720
The other option is to actually synchronize the data

72
00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.720
using the data sync service onto a cloud Amazon S3 bucket,

73
00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.500
where your instances can access the data

74
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:33.180
directly from there.

75
00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.710
So these are the two options,

76
00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:38.520
and it's one example on how to use AWS Outposts.

77
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.200
So that's it, I hope you liked it

78
00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.150
and I will see you in the next lecture.

